## [ISSUE] A per-client permission hides every all-clients permission for that client

**Severity:** HIGH — creating any permission for one client takes away that client's access from admins who were granted access through an "all clients" permission.

### The Problem

```java
Resource resource =  resourceStore.findByName(server, client.getId(), server.getId());
if (resource == null) {
    // check if there is permission for "all-clients". ...
    resource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
    ...
}
Collection<Permission> permissions = root.evaluatePermission(new ResourcePermission(resource, resource.getScopes(), server), server);
```

The code evaluates either the per-client resource or the `Clients` type resource, never both. The all-clients resource is used only when the per-client resource is missing.

### Theory / Proof

- `AuthorizationProvider` policy creation (`server-spi-private/.../AuthorizationProvider.java:302-327`) links a permission that names specific clients to the per-client resource. A permission without resources is linked to the `Clients` type resource.
- `AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource` (line 104) creates the per-client resource with `resourceStore.create(resourceServer, name, owner)` and never sets a `type`.
- `DefaultPolicyEvaluator.evaluate` (`DefaultPolicyEvaluator.java:70-88`) loads policies from `findByResource(resource)`. It calls `findByResourceType` only when `resource.getType() != null`. `findByScopes(server, null, ...)` returns only policies that have **no** resource (`findPolicyIdByNullResourceScope`). So when the per-client resource is evaluated, nothing links it to policies on the `Clients` resource.

### What happens today vs. what will break

1. Admin A gets `manage` on all clients. Admin A can manage client X because X has no resource yet.
2. Someone creates a permission "view client X" for admin B. This creates resource `X.getId()`.
3. Now `canManage(X)` for admin A only evaluates the view permission on X's resource and returns false. Admin A gets 403 on X, and a client they can still manage looks like one they cannot.

The new tests never combine an all-clients permission with a per-client permission (`PermissionClientTest.testManageAllClients` and `testManageOnlyOneClient` each use one kind), so this is not caught. `UserPermissionsV2.hasPermission` uses the same pattern, so users have the same bug, but this PR copies it to clients.

### Research

In the V2 FGAP design, a type-level permission is meant to act as a default that per-resource permissions refine. The Javadoc added in this PR says so as well: "For V2 only: Also if the caller has a permission to MANAGE **all clients**". The current code cannot deliver that "also".

### Recommendation

Evaluate both resources and grant if either gives the scope, or give the per-client resource the type so the evaluator also picks up type policies:

```java
private boolean hasPermission(ClientModel client, String scope) {
    if (!root.isAdminSameRealm()) return false;
    ResourceServer server = root.realmResourceServer();
    if (server == null) return false;

    Resource specific = resourceStore.findByName(server, client.getId(), server.getId());
    if (specific != null && evaluate(server, specific, scope)) {
        return true;
    }
    Resource all = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
    return all != null && evaluate(server, all, scope);
}
```

Also add a test that creates an all-clients `manage` permission for admin A plus a per-client `view` permission for admin B on the same client, then checks that admin A can still manage it.

## [GAP ANALYSIS] `canView()` ignores all-clients `manage`, so a manage-all admin cannot view client scopes

**Severity:** MEDIUM — the permissions do not match: an admin can create, delete and manage client scopes but gets 403 when reading them.

### The Problem

```java
@Override
public boolean canView() {
    return canViewClientDefault() || hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW);
}
```

Meanwhile, in the same class:

```java
public boolean canManage() { return super.canManage() || hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE); }
public boolean canManageClientScopes() { ... return hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE); }
public boolean canView(ClientScopeModel clientScope) {
    ...
    return hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW) || hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```

`canView(ClientScopeModel)` treats MANAGE as implying VIEW, but `canView()` does not.

### Theory / Proof

In `ClientPermissions`, all of these depend only on `canView()`: `canViewClientScopes()` (line 489), `canListClientScopes()` (line 258), and `canList()` (line 241, apart from query roles). They are used by:
- `ClientScopesResource.getClientScopes` -> `requireListClientScopes()` (line 91). This returns 403 unless the admin has `query-clients`.
- `RealmAdminResource.getDefaultClientScopes` -> `requireViewClientScopes()` (line 241).
- `ClientInitialAccessResource` (line 123) and `RealmAdminResource` (line 1197) -> `requireView()`.
- `ClientsResource.getClients` -> `canView()` (line 122). This picks between the paged query and checking every client with `canView(c)`.

The role-based defaults follow the rule that manage implies view: `canViewClientDefault()` accepts `MANAGE_CLIENTS`. `UserPermissionsV2.canView` checks both `VIEW` and `MANAGE`.

### What happens today vs. what will break

An admin with only an all-clients `manage` permission (and no `query-clients`) can `POST /clients` and `POST /client-scopes`, but `GET /client-scopes` and `GET /default-default-client-scopes` return 403. The test user `myadmin` has `query-users`, so `testManageAllClients` still passes `requireList()` and does not catch this.

### Recommendation

```java
@Override
public boolean canView() {
    return canViewClientDefault()
            || hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW)
            || hasPermission(AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```

Better still, let `hasPermission(String... scopes)` accept varargs, as `UserPermissionsV2` does, so this takes a single evaluation.

## [ISSUE] `getClientsWithPermission` never finds per-client resources and can return the string `"Clients"`

**Severity:** MEDIUM — the method is wrong for every input. The one caller does not reach it under V2 today, but it will crash if that gate changes.

### The Problem

```java
resourceStore.findByType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE, resource -> {
    if (hasGrantedPermission(resource, scope)) {
        granted.add(resource.getName());
    }
});
```

### Theory / Proof

- Per-client resources come from `AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource` -> `resourceStore.create(resourceServer, name, owner)` (line 104). This never calls `setType`, so `findByType(..., "Clients")` does not match them.
- The only resource with type `Clients` is the type resource created in `AdminPermissionsSchema.init` (`resource.setType(type)`, line 247). Its name is `"Clients"`.
- So the method returns either `{}` or `{"Clients"}`, never a client UUID.
- The only caller, `AvailableRoleMappingResource.getRoleIdsWithPermissions` (`rest/admin-ui-ext/.../AvailableRoleMappingResource.java:229-230`), runs `realm.getClientById(cid).getRolesStream()`. With `cid = "Clients"`, `getClientById` returns null and the call throws an NPE.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Today the caller only takes this branch when `Profile.Feature.ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ` (V1) is enabled. V1 and V2 share the unversioned key, so they cannot both be on, and the V2 override is never called. Once the admin UI role-mapping endpoints are adapted for V2 (the obvious next step for #35564), the NPE and the missing per-client results show up at once.

### Research

The V1 version avoids this because V1 resources are created with `type = "Client"` and names prefixed `client.resource.`, which it strips (`ClientPermissions.java:680-683`). The V2 schema changed both conventions and this method was not updated for either.

### Recommendation

Collect the per-client resources that are attached to Clients-type permissions, and handle the all-clients case separately:

```java
Set<String> granted = new HashSet<>();
Resource all = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
if (all != null && hasGrantedPermission(all, scope)) {
    realm.getClientsStream().map(ClientModel::getId).forEach(granted::add);
    return granted;
}
policyStore.findByResourceType(server, CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE).stream()
        .flatMap(p -> p.getResources().stream())
        .filter(r -> !r.getName().equals(CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE))
        .filter(r -> hasGrantedPermission(r, scope))
        .forEach(r -> granted.add(r.getName()));
return granted;
```

Or throw `UnsupportedOperationException`, like the other V2 overrides that are not implemented yet, so a wrong result cannot come back silently.

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Under V2, deleting a client leaves its FGAP resource and permissions behind

**Severity:** LOW — orphaned authorization data builds up, and permissions that point at deleted clients stay visible in the admin console.

### The Problem

```java
if (Profile.isFeatureEnabled(Profile.Feature.ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ)) {
    ...
    } else if (event instanceof ClientModel.ClientRemovedEvent) {
        ... .clients().setPermissionsEnabled(cast.getClient(), false);
```

The wrapper is needed because `ClientPermissionsV2.setPermissionsEnabled` now throws `UnsupportedOperationException`. However, nothing replaces the cleanup for V2.

### Theory / Proof

- V2 per-client resources are named with the client UUID and owned by the admin-permissions resource server (`AdminPermissionsSchema.getOrCreateResource`, line 104).
- `ClientApplicationSynchronizer` (`server-spi-private/.../syncronization/ClientApplicationSynchronizer.java:49-75`) only deletes the removed client's own resource server and edits `client`-type policies. It does not touch resources named after the client in other resource servers.
- `AdminPermissionsSchema.removeOrphanResources` only runs when a policy is deleted, not when a client is deleted.

### What happens today vs. what will break

After `DELETE /clients/{id}`, the `Clients` scope permission still lists a resource named with the deleted UUID. Permissions that only targeted that client become empty shells that nothing can match. Client UUIDs are never reused, so this does not grant anyone access. The cost is stale permissions in the UI and in exports, and in `getClientsWithPermission` IDs that no longer resolve (see the NPE path there).

### Recommendation

Add a V2 branch that removes the resource the same way `UserSynchronizer.removeUserResources` does:

```java
} else if (Profile.isFeatureEnabled(Profile.Feature.ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ_V2)
        && event instanceof ClientModel.ClientRemovedEvent cast) {
    AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.removeResourceObject(cast.getKeycloakSession(), cast.getClient().getRealm(), cast.getClient().getId());
}
```

Here `removeResourceObject` looks up the resource by name on the realm's admin-permissions resource server. It deletes each policy whose only resource is this one, and otherwise removes the resource from the policy.
